[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The campus access and distribution fabric for Office A is healthy. From PC1 (10.1.0.100) we can reach PC2 (10.1.0.101) with 0% loss and ~1 ms RTT, and the reverse path is also clean. Both PCs can also ping the VLAN 10 gateway IPs (10.1.0.1/10.1.0.2/10.1.0.3). ARP tables on both hosts show valid neighbor entries, and the MAC tables on the access switches confirm correct L2 learning: on aswa2, PC1’s MAC (52:03:82:89:01:96) is learned on eth0 (access to PC1) VLAN 10 and PC2’s MAC (7a:ac:57:7b:7d:31) is learned via the trunk (eth1) VLAN 10; on aswa3 the converse is true. Distribution switches DSWA1/DSWA2 interfaces (uplinks and inter‑DSW bonds) are UP with no errors or drops, and VLAN 10 SVI (br0.10) is present on both, indicating the gateway redundancy is intact.

Mechanism: If DSWA1 or an uplink had failed, we would see loss, missing FDB/ARP entries, or downed ports; none are present. Localization: Access aswa2.eth0 (PC1), aswa3.eth0 (PC2), as well as trunks to DSWA1/DSWA2 are operational. Root cause: No current network fault reproduced; the reported outage was transient or host-side and resolved before investigation.

Corrective fix: No device or link restart is required. If the symptom recurs, first flush ARP on the affected PC(s) or bounce the access port (aswa2 eth0 for PC1, aswa3 eth0 for PC2) to clear stale state, then monitor. 
=== END ===